  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe current SLSQP-based approach struggles with local optima and inadequate exploration. We propose a multi-phase evolutionary strategy combining global exploration with local refinement:\n\nPhase 1: Coarse search with lower resolution (n=35) to quickly identify promising regions\nPhase 2: Fine search at higher resolution (n=70) using best candidates from Phase 1\nPhase 3: Local optimization using SLSQP on top Phase 2 candidates\n\nKey improvements:\n- Avoids local optima traps via population-based search\n- Leverages coarse-to-fine search strategy\n- Maintains feasible solutions throughout\n- Explicitly handles the non-convex correlation objective\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nObjective: Minimize C\u2085 = max_k \u03a3 h_i (1 - h_{i+k}) dx\nConstraints:\n- 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n- \u03a3 h_i dx = 1 (exactly)\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n1. Initialize population with diversified candidates\n2. Evolutionary search:\n   a. Selection: Tournament selection on C5\n   b. Crossover: Blend parent features\n   c. Mutation: Gaussian perturbations with decaying variance\n   d. Local refinement: SLSQP on top candidates\n3. Adaptive resolution: Start coarse, upsample best candidates\n4. Maintain feasibility via projection and rescaling\n5. Monitor progress and time budget\n6. Return best candidate\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Population size: 50\n- Mutation std: 0.1 \u2192 0.01 over iterations\n- Crossover blend \u03b1: 0.2-0.8\n- Elitism: Keep top 10%\n- Local search frequency: 10% of population\n- Resolution schedule: 35 \u2192 70 points\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Evolution steps dominated by population evaluation\n- Each evaluation: O(n\u00b2) for correlation\n- Time budget estimate: \n   - Coarse phase: ~200s\n   - Fine phase: ~600s\n   - Final refinement: ~100s\n- Robust to local optima through population diversity\n- Handles infeasible proposals via projection\n- Maintains progress tracking for early exit\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Ensure h \u2208 [0,1] and sum(h) = n/2\n- Verify C5 via direct evaluator\n- Check finite values and feasibility\n- Compare against initial solution",
